<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Carchemish</h1><p class="st-meta">1180 BCE – 717 BCE (approx.) · capital Carchemish</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Neo-Hittite Carchemish, the leading Iron Age kingdom on the Euphrates, heir to Hittite royal tradition and famed for its sculpted walls.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> fell c. 1180 BCE, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viceroyalty-of-carchemish">Viceroyalty of Carchemish</a> survived, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuzi-teshub">Kuzi-Teshub</a> took the title great king. His descendants and their successors ruled a kingdom astride the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> crossing, in what is now the Turkish-Syrian border.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings of the house of Suhi, and later Astiru and the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yariri">Yariri</a>, recorded their deeds in Luwian hieroglyphs on basalt reliefs lining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/processional-way">processional ways</a>, the Long Wall of Sculpture. Carchemish grew rich on river trade and textiles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> excavations from 1911, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Leonard Woolley</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-e-lawrence">T. E. Lawrence</a>, uncovered much of the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The kingdom paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> from the 9th century BCE. In 717 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> deposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisiri">Pisiri</a> for plotting with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-phrygia">Phrygia</a> and turned Carchemish into a province. In 605 BCE the city saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar's</a> victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1180 BCE – 717 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:36.22%;width:9.21%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1180 BCE – 717 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Carchemish</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Suhi II, Katuwa, Pisiri</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Luwian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Luwian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Carchemish, Neo-Hittite Carchemish</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-948&amp;focus=kingdom-of-carchemish" aria-label="Kingdom of Carchemish on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5634 850 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Carchemish lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5954 1106L5954 1106M5954 1106L6092 1134"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5954 1106h0M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5954 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5954 1106h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Carchemish. <a href="/atlas?year=-948&amp;focus=kingdom-of-carchemish">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
